<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Check a chest-vibration forecast · Interoceptomics</title>
<style>
:root{color-scheme:light;font:16px/1.5 "Inter",Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#203733;background:#f5f7f2}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0}main{max-width:1020px;margin:auto;padding:20px clamp(14px,4vw,54px) 36px}nav,footer{font-size:.88rem;color:#50635e}nav{margin-bottom:1.2rem}a{color:#205f58;text-underline-offset:.18em}h1{font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,3.9rem);line-height:1.05;letter-spacing:-.055em;margin:0 0 .8rem;max-width:20ch}h2{font-size:1.32rem;line-height:1.25;margin:1.3rem 0 .4rem}p{margin:.45rem 0 1rem;max-width:78ch}label{font-weight:650}button,select,input{font:inherit}button,select{padding:.45rem .72rem;border-radius:.3rem;border:1px solid #6b8880;background:white;color:inherit}button{cursor:pointer}button:hover{background:#e6eee7}:focus-visible{outline:3px solid #9b6022;outline-offset:2px}.controls,.row,.legend{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;gap:.5rem 1rem}.controls{border-top:2px solid #45635b;padding-top:1rem;margin-top:1.3rem}.row{justify-content:space-between}.small,.legend,figcaption{font-size:.86rem;color:#475d55}.legend{gap:.35rem .9rem}.legend span::before{content:'';border-top:3px solid var(--col);display:inline-block;width:18px;vertical-align:middle;margin-right:4px}.trace{--col:#2b5250}.aligned{--col:#146ab1}.base{--col:#a34428}.target{--col:#6d3b81}figure{margin:.7rem 0;background:white;border:1px solid #cad6ce;padding:.5rem .65rem}.plot{width:100%;height:clamp(170px,22vw,210px);display:block}input[type=range]{width:100%;accent-color:#245b53}input[type=file]{max-width:100%;display:block;margin:.5rem 0}.tables{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:24px}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.94rem}th,td{padding:.38rem .25rem;text-align:left;border-bottom:1px solid #c4cfc7}td:last-child,th:last-child{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:650}details{margin-top:1.25rem}footer{margin-top:1.4rem;border-top:1px solid #becbc3;padding-top:.8rem}button[aria-pressed=true]{background:#e0ebe4}output{font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}#message{color:#693719;font-weight:600;min-height:0}#message:empty{display:none}[hidden]{display:none!important}@media(max-width:630px){.tables{grid-template-columns:1fr;gap:0}.row{align-items:flex-start}.row>*{min-width:0}main{padding-top:14px}}
</style></head><body><main><nav><a href="index.html">Heart and breathing recordings</a> · <a href="forecast.html">Try an invented pulse</a></nav>
<h1>Can you forecast the next chest vibration?</h1><p>Stop a recording just before the chest sensor takes another reading. One model knows the recent chest vibration and breathing. The other also knows the heart’s electrical signal. Move the cutoff, then reveal what the chest sensor recorded 80 milliseconds later.</p>
<section aria-label="Recorded test"><div class="controls"><label for="record">Test recording</label><select id="record"></select><span id="coverage" class="small"></span></div><p id="message" role="status"></p>
<div id="experiment"><div class="row"><label for="cut">Forecast cutoff on the recording clock</label><output id="clock" for="cut"></output></div><input id="cut" type="range" step="1" min="0" max="249" value="0">
<figure><canvas class="plot" id="plot" aria-label="Past chest vibration and forecast points" role="img"></canvas><figcaption>The vertical line marks the last sensor input. Read the exact forecasts and the measured value in the table.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="legend"><span class="trace">Earlier vibration</span><span class="base">Chest and breathing</span><span class="aligned">With ECG</span><span class="target" id="target-key" hidden>Later measurement</span></div>
<div class="tables"><div><h2>At <output id="target-time"></output></h2><table aria-label="Predicted and measured sensor values"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Input or reading</th><th scope="col">Sensor mV</th></tr></thead><tbody id="values"></tbody></table><button id="reveal" aria-pressed="false">Reveal the later reading</button><p id="comparison" class="small"></p></div>
<div><h2>Over this recording</h2><p id="score-scope" class="small"></p><table aria-label="Root mean squared error in millivolts"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Forecast</th><th scope="col">Error (mV)</th></tr></thead><tbody id="scores"></tbody></table></div></div>
</div></section>
<details><summary>Open the full 30-second test interval</summary><p>Each built-in example contains the first second of test cutoffs. To inspect all 7,500 cutoffs for a recording, download its original waveform from PhysioNet and open the file here. The browser checks the file against the named archive, reads it on your device and runs the fitted models.</p>
<p><a id="source-file" href="https://physionet.org/content/cebsdb/1.0.0/">Open the original waveform on PhysioNet</a></p><label for="local">Original CEBSDB .dat file</label><input id="local" type="file" accept=".dat"><button id="short">Restore one-second example</button></details>
<details><summary>What the numbers measure</summary><p>ECG lead II records electrical activity through skin electrodes. A band follows breathing and an accelerometer on the chest picks up vibration. All numbers here are sensor output in millivolts. Each displayed chest reading combines the preceding 40 milliseconds of raw samples; the target combines only samples after the cutoff, from +40 to +80 milliseconds.</p><p>The models learned from five people. These are the five other recordings used to test them. The calibration for each person uses their own earlier readings between 60 and 80 seconds. “Earlier ECG” uses the electrical signal from 5.012 seconds earlier, with its own fitted coefficients. The error table gives the root mean squared error for the visible interval. Smaller is better.</p><p>Across the full test intervals, adding ECG helped two people and worsened the average error by 8.76% relative to each person’s chest-and-breathing model. One short excerpt or a single forecast can give a different impression. These sensors do not record how a heartbeat felt.</p></details>
<footer>CEBSDB version 1.0.0. Miguel Ángel García González and Ariadna Argelagós Palou, Universitat Politècnica de Catalunya, via PhysioNet. The excerpts were filtered, calibrated and shortened. <a href="https://doi.org/10.13026/C2KW23">Original database</a> · <a href="https://physionet.org/content/cebsdb/view-license/1.0.0/">ODC Attribution License 1.0</a> · <a href="https://github.com/eliminatenoise/Interoceptomics/tree/main/models/chest-vibration">Code, credits and full results</a></footer>
<noscript>Enable JavaScript to calculate forecasts. The linked study includes a Python predictor.</noscript>
</main><script type="application/json" id="bundle">/* DATA */</script><script>/* INFERENCE */</script><script>/* VIEW */</script></body></html>
